<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 20px; text-align: center;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # It's the Best! – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Grade:</b> 4th Grade (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Scenario:</b> A Walk in the Neighborhood</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Theme:</b> It's the Best!</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Respond to questions about familiar topics.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can listen and identify main points in talks about favorite places in the neighborhood (e.g., 'The park has swings.').<br>Can respond to questions about familiar places (e.g., 'What is your favorite place?' 'My favorite place is the park.').<br>Can listen to and recognize the main points people share about places they like (e.g., 'I like the store. It has toys.').</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333; background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 34%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows flashcards or pictures of different places in a neighborhood (e.g., park, street, building, playground, tree, flower). The teacher asks, "What do you see?" and elicits simple responses like "A park!" or "A tree!" The teacher then introduces the lesson theme: "Today we will talk about our favorite places in the neighborhood!"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a picture of a park and says, "This is a park. I like this park. It is fun! It has swings. This park is the <b>best</b>!" The teacher models the phrase "I like this [place] because it is [adjective]." and "This [place] is the <b>best</b>." The teacher emphasizes the /b/ sound in 'best' and the /f/ sound in 'favorite' as they say, "My <b>favorite</b> place is the park." The teacher also claps out syllables for 'big,' 'bigger,' 'biggest' using gestures to show size, introducing the concept of superlatives.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher uses visuals and gestures to check understanding of new vocabulary and concepts.<br>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">CCQ 1: (Pointing to a picture of a park) "Is this a house or a park?" (Expected answer: "A park!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">CCQ 2: (After saying "This park is the best!") "Does 'best' mean good or bad?" (Expected answer: "Good!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">CCQ 3: (Showing a big tree and a small flower) "Which one is 'big'?" (Students point). "Which one is the 'biggest'?" (Students point, connecting to phonemic awareness).</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "A Walk in the Neighborhood")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Short audio recording (1-1.5 minutes) of two children, Ana and Leo, describing their favorite places during "A Walk in the Neighborhood." The dialogue uses target vocabulary (e.g., park, playground, street, tree, flower, building) and grammar (superlatives, simple present + because).<br>Example sentences from audio: "I like the park because it is fun. It has the <b>best</b> swings!" "My <b>favorite</b> place is the quiet street because it has beautiful trees." "I enjoy the big playground because I can run and play."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Interpreting spoken messages about familiar topics and everyday situations.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher plays the audio once. <b>Gist Question:</b> "What are Ana and Leo talking about? Places in school or places in the neighborhood?" (Students raise hand for correct answer).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher plays the audio a second time. Students listen and complete a simple "True or False" activity on their worksheets with two statements, e.g., "Ana likes the park. (T/F)" and "Leo likes the noisy street. (T/F)".</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher plays the audio a third time, pausing after each speaker. Students match the speaker (Ana/Leo) to their favorite place (Park/Street) from two pictures on their worksheet.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Listening)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice listening for specific details, using target grammar (Superlatives: 'best', Simple present + conjunction: 'because', Question structure: 'What is your favorite?'), and vocabulary (neighborhood, park, playground, street, fun, beautiful, favorite). Pronunciation focus on /b/ in 'best' and /f/ in 'favorite', and phonemic awareness of blending /b/ and /ɪg/ to form 'big', 'bigger', 'biggest'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 1 (Controlled Practice - Listening for Specifics):</b> The teacher distributes a worksheet with sentences from the audio that have missing key vocabulary words. Students listen to the audio again (sentence by sentence or short chunks) and fill in the blanks. E.g., "I like the _______ because it is fun." (park). "It has the ______ swings!" (best). Teacher models the words 'best' and 'favorite' emphasizing /b/ and /f/ sounds.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 2 (Semi-controlled - Listen and Choose):</b> The teacher shows three pictures of neighborhood places (e.g., a quiet street, a busy playground, a beautiful park with flowers). The teacher plays short descriptions (e.g., "I like the quiet street because it has beautiful trees.") and students circle the picture that matches what they hear. This activity reinforces vocabulary and listening for descriptive adjectives. Teacher monitors for understanding and pronunciation.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 3 (Pair Interaction - Listen and Confirm):</b> Students work in pairs. The teacher gives each pair a set of small picture cards of neighborhood places (park, street, building, playground). The teacher says a sentence, e.g., "My <b>favorite</b> place is the park because it is <b>big</b> and has many <b>flowers</b>." One student listens and picks up the correct picture card. The other student listens and confirms, "Yes, that's the park." The teacher then prompts, "Say it: 'big', 'bigger', 'biggest'," to reinforce phonemic awareness.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">15-18 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "A Walk in the Neighborhood")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format: Listen and Draw/Identify "My Best Place"</b><br>
                Students receive a blank worksheet with an outline of a neighborhood map (empty spaces for places). The teacher describes their "favorite" or "best" neighborhood place, using the target vocabulary, grammar, and pronunciation. Students listen carefully and draw or label what the teacher describes on their map. The description will include reasons using 'because'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Description Example:</b> "Welcome to my neighborhood! My <b>favorite</b> place is the big park. I like the park <b>because</b> it has the <b>best</b> swings! There are many beautiful <b>flowers</b> and tall <b>trees</b> in my park. It's the <b>biggest</b> park on my street. Sometimes I see birds. It's a very fun place. What is my <b>favorite</b> place? Draw it on your map!"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Students' listening comprehension of descriptions, vocabulary, superlatives ('best', 'biggest'), and 'because' statements. The task encourages active listening for details and meaning to accurately represent the described place.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">8-10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation during the "Listen and Draw/Identify" activity, using a simple checklist to note students' ability to follow listening instructions and identify key elements.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students complete a short listening quiz. The teacher plays 2-3 short audio sentences (similar to the Presentation stage) about different favorite neighborhood places. For each sentence, students circle the correct picture (from 2 options) that matches the description they hear. E.g., "I like the playground because it has the best slide!" (Students choose between a picture of a playground or a street).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accuracy of chosen pictures based on listening comprehension.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible identification of key vocabulary (e.g., 'park', 'playground', 'street').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Recognition of descriptive adjectives and superlatives (e.g., 'fun', 'beautiful', 'best').</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete two sentences on a small paper slip before leaving.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Today I can listen and understand _________________________ (e.g., favorite places, why people like places)."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"My favorite word I heard today was _________________________."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy to listen to today? What was difficult?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new words did you hear about the neighborhood?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this listening-focused lesson? (e.g., clear audio, visuals, repetition).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolds are needed for students who struggled with identifying main points or specific vocabulary during listening tasks? (e.g., pre-teaching more vocabulary, providing sentence frames).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Were the pronunciation points (/b/ and /f/, 'big,' 'bigger,' 'biggest') effectively integrated and practiced?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> "Great job listening today! Next time, we will read a story about a walk in the neighborhood using many of the words we heard today, like 'park' and 'playground', and find out what makes them 'the best'!"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">2-3 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>